---
/**
 * How this was assembled, and what checks it passed.
 *
 * Every figure on the page is relative to one prompt version and one query
 * plan, and both are here, because the difference between "there is no agentic
 * work in hydrogeology" and "nothing we ran looked properly" is only visible
 * from the query list. Absence claims in the report cite query ids for exactly
 * this reason, and the ids resolve here.
 *
 * The audit table is the part worth reading. It is generated by
 * `scripts/audit.py`, which parses the run's own artifacts and exits non-zero
 * on failure — 26 executed checks, including whether every citation resolves,
 * whether the core tier was actually deep-read, and whether any substantive
 * paragraph is missing an evidence tag. It replaced a self-attested audit
 * table, which asked a long compacted run to verify its own compliance from
 * memory. Showing the generated table rather than a claim of quality is the
 * same argument one level up.
 *
 * The derived-framework keyword list is disclosed here in full. It is the only
 * thing on the page that is not either read from an artifact or counted from
 * one, so it is the only thing a reader has to take on trust — and the way to
 * charge less trust for it is to print it.
 */
import type { Dataset } from '../lib/types';

interface Props {
  data: Dataset;
}
const { data } = Astro.props;

const run = data.run;
const pretty = (s: string) => s.replace(/_/g, ' ');
const num = (n: number | null) => (n === null ? '—' : n.toLocaleString('en-GB'));

const failed = data.audit.checks.filter((c) => c.result !== 'PASS');
// One row per query group rather than per call: each group is an OpenAlex call
// and an arXiv call over the same domain, and 71 rows of near-identical
// boolean strings is a wall nobody reads.
const groups = [...new Set(data.queries.map((q) => q.group))];
---

<div class="section-head">
  <h2>How this was assembled</h2>
  <p>
    One run, one prompt version, one query plan. Everything above is relative to what is
    below — read the query plan before concluding that a field is empty, because it may
    only mean that nothing here went looking.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="two-up">
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="head">
      <h3>Run record</h3>
      <span class="muted">{run.version}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <dl class="kv">
        <dt>run directory</dt>
        <dd><code>{run.path}</code></dd>
        <dt>prompt</dt>
        <dd>{run.prompt || <span class="na">not stated</span>}</dd>
        <dt>model</dt>
        <dd>{run.model || <span class="na">not stated</span>}</dd>
        <dt>purpose</dt>
        <dd>{run.purpose || <span class="na">not stated</span>}</dd>
        <dt>date</dt>
        <dd>{run.date || <span class="na">not stated</span>}</dd>
        <dt>harvested</dt>
        <dd>{num(run.harvested)} unique records</dd>
        <dt>shortlisted</dt>
        <dd>{num(run.shortlisted)} after deterministic triage</dd>
        <dt>screened</dt>
        <dd>{num(run.screened)} in-or-out decisions</dd>
        <dt>admitted</dt>
        <dd>{num(run.admitted)} — {run.core} core, {run.context} context</dd>
        <dt>recall audit</dt>
        <dd>
          {run.false_negatives} false {run.false_negatives === 1 ? 'negative' : 'negatives'} in
          {' '}{run.audit_sample} records screened from below the triage cut
          {run.audit_sample > 0 && ` (${((run.false_negatives / run.audit_sample) * 100).toFixed(1)}%)`}
        </dd>
        <dt>calls</dt>
        <dd>
          {run.api_calls} total — {run.queries_core} core-scoped, {run.queries_periphery} periphery-scoped,
          {' '}{run.api_errors} errored
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <div class="head">
      <h3>Contract audit</h3>
      <span class={failed.length === 0 ? 'pass' : 'fail'}>
        {data.audit.passed}/{data.audit.checks.length} pass
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 1rem">
        Generated by <code>scripts/audit.py</code>, which parses the run's artifacts and
        exits non-zero on failure. Nothing here is self-attested.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="grid">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Check</th>
              <th>Result</th>
              <th>Detail</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              data.audit.checks.map((c) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{c.check}</td>
                  <td class={c.result === 'PASS' ? 'pass' : 'fail'}>{c.result}</td>
                  <td style="color:var(--muted)">{c.detail}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="panel" style="margin-top:1rem">
  <div class="head">
    <h3>Query plan</h3>
    <span class="muted">{data.queries.length} calls over {groups.length} domain groups</span>
  </div>
  <div class="body">
    <details class="tail">
      <summary>every call, with what the API said was available</summary>
      <div class="scroll-x" style="margin-top:1rem">
        <table class="grid">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>id</th>
              <th>api</th>
              <th>scope</th>
              <th>group</th>
              <th>band</th>
              <th class="num">available</th>
              <th class="num">returned</th>
              <th class="num">new</th>
              <th>status</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              data.queries.map((q) => (
                <tr>
                  <td style="white-space:nowrap"><code>{q.id}</code></td>
                  <td>{q.api}</td>
                  <td>{q.scope}</td>
                  <td>{pretty(q.group)}</td>
                  <td style="color:var(--muted)">{q.band}</td>
                  <td class="num">{q.available === null ? '—' : q.available.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</td>
                  <td class="num">{q.results === null ? '—' : q.results.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</td>
                  <td class="num">{q.new === null ? '—' : q.new.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</td>
                  <td class={q.status === 'ok' ? 'pass' : 'fail'}>{q.status}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:1rem 0 0">
        <code>available</code> is the API's own result count, logged so that a paging cap
        is visible in the artifact rather than being reported downstream as the size of a
        literature. Raising those caps mid-run is why this is v0.5 and not v0.4.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="panel" style="margin-top:1rem">
  <div class="head">
    <h3>The one derived facet</h3>
    <span class="muted">not a SCHEMA column, not audited</span>
  </div>
  <div class="body">
    <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 1.5rem">
      Everything else on this page is read from a run artifact or counted from one. The
      framework facet is a keyword match over the free-text <code>tools_used</code> and
      <code>base_model</code> cells, grouped by kind. The list is fixed in
      <code>scripts/export_web.py</code>; a tool no paper named cannot appear, and a paper
      that named a tool it merely cited is counted as calling it. Treat a count as a floor.
    </p>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 1.5rem">
      Every term below is a term the match looks for. The greyed ones matched nothing in
      this run, and the page cannot tell you whether that is a tool this corpus does not
      use or a pattern that does not work — so it shows you the term. Two greyed terms in
      an earlier build were the second kind:
      <code>\bSPECFEM\b</code> could not match <code>SPECFEM2D</code>, and the harness
      terms were only being matched against the wrong column.
    </p>
    <div class="three-up">
      {
        [...new Set(data.framework_terms.map((t) => t.kind))].map((kind) => (
          <div>
            <h4 class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:.6rem">
              {kind}
            </h4>
            <div class="chips">
              {data.framework_terms
                .filter((t) => t.kind === kind)
                .map((t) => (
                  <span
                    class="chip"
                    style={t.matched ? '' : 'color:var(--faint);background:none;box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--hair-2)'}
                    title={t.matched ? 'matched at least one source' : 'matched nothing in this run'}
                  >
                    {t.label}
                  </span>
                ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:1.25rem 0 0">
      {data.framework_terms.filter((t) => t.matched).length} of {data.framework_terms.length}
      terms matched at least one source.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
